{
  "program": "J",
  "title": "Block width controls both lag-search power and periodic-plaintext false alarms",
  "hard": false,
  "on_path": true,
  "statement": "Across three additional historical texts and three row seeds per text, block widths 16 and 25 confirm all nine planted period-10 cases but also confirm nine and eight of nine ten-character repeated-plaintext cases with random cipher rows. Widths 40, 50, 80 and 100 confirm none of either set. All six widths confirm all nine planted period-24 and period-131 cases. Width 25 sometimes confirms a multiple of the plaintext motif instead of lag 10. These controls expose an alignment-sensitive power/false-signal tradeoff, not a validated manuscript null.",
  "check": "Vary block offsets, noisy and drifting schedules, unrelated longer-range plaintext structures, row reuse and more independent books; explicitly define which dependencies are null and validate power and false-alarm rates under that null. Do not use these cases alone to select a manuscript hypothesis or rule out a cipher.",
  "method": "Freeze the P-J-042 scan and confirmation rule: exact word equality at lags 2\u2013160, 49 block-permutation maximum null replicates for choosing the shortest significant lag on the first 4,000 words, then 49 fixed-lag block permutations on the next 4,000 words. Permute intact consecutive blocks of widths 16,25,40,50,80,100, all dividing 4,000 exactly. Use distinct pinned French Roman de la Rose, English Chaucer and Spanish El conde Lucanor, normalized by the reference corpus; characters 4,001\u201312,000 in the Hermes table alphabet. For each text, three per-book-offset seeds select distinct cyclic rows at periods 10,24,131. An independently sampled 10-character motif with independently random row selection supplies a structured plaintext control. Inputs and table are hash-verified.",
  "limitations": "Each source passage is reused for three seeds and six widths; period and block settings share data and are correlated. Texts have been used elsewhere in the project. Block offsets were fixed at segment starts; other offsets, transcription noise and schedule variation remain untested. A block null that preserves part of a signal may lose power, and smaller blocks can preserve too little plaintext structure. No manuscript data or key recovery is tested.",
  "environment": "Python and NumPy 2.5.4; Hermes table https://github.com/hermesj/R_Voynich_Stats/blob/0ef1439fa9d90aeab42951a51d0d5a0e27db1f8f/R%20code/Historical_ciphers/keys/ciphers_poly_III_in_order.csv, SHA-256 fe3f24a46dbc6b655eab8dbbd75addf84ee4a7dbd387ad802af75d02edfe5b4d.",
